# Day 4 — Active Directory User Lifecycle, File Sharing & Access Troubleshooting
## Objective
The objective of Day 4 was to simulate common Tier 1/2 IT administration tasks involving employee onboarding, Active Directory security groups, network file shares, Group Policy drive mapping, access troubleshooting, and employee offboarding.
A fictional employee was provisioned in Active Directory, granted departmental access through group membership, provided a network drive automatically through Group Policy, intentionally subjected to an access failure, and ultimately offboarded.
This exercise demonstrated the difference between authentication, authorization, Group Policy configuration, and Windows security group membership.

## Environment
| System | Hostname | Role |
|---|---|---|
| Proxmox | MS01 | Virtualization host |
| Windows Server 2022 | WS01 | Active Directory / DNS / File Server |
| Windows 11 Pro | AC02 | Domain-joined workstation |
| Ubuntu Server | UBV01 | Linux server |
| Linux Mint | OPS01 | Administration workstation |
**Domain:** `blackwood.local`  
**NetBIOS Domain:** `BLACKWOOD`

## 1. Departmental Active Directory Structure
Created a new Organizational Unit:
```text
Operations
```
This OU was used to organize user objects belonging to the fictional Operations department.
A new security group was also created inside the existing Groups OU:
```text
Operations-Users
```
Configuration:
```text
Group scope: Global
Group type:  Security
```
This demonstrated an important distinction:
```text
Organizational Unit
    → Organizes and manages AD objects
Security Group
    → Assigns permissions/access
```
Rather than assigning resource permissions directly to individual users, departmental access was designed around security group membership.

## 2. Employee Onboarding
Created a fictional employee account:
```text
Name:     Jordan Reed
Username: jreed
UPN:      jreed@blackwood.local
```
The account was created inside the Operations OU.
A temporary lab-only password was assigned and:
```text
User must change password at next logon
```
was enabled.
This simulated an onboarding workflow in which IT provisions an initial account while requiring the employee to establish a new password during first login.
Jordan was then added to:
```text
Operations-Users
```
Group membership was verified on WS01 using PowerShell:
```powershell
Get-ADUser jreed -Properties MemberOf | Select-Object -ExpandProperty MemberOf
```
The resulting membership included:
```text
CN=Operations-Users,OU=Groups,DC=blackwood,DC=local
```

## 3. Departmental Network Share
Created a departmental directory on WS01:
```text
C:\Shares\Operations
```
The directory was shared as:
```text
\\WS01\Operations
```
A test document was placed inside the share:
```text
Operations-Welcome.txt
```
### Share Permissions
Rather than granting Jordan direct access, permissions were assigned to:
```text
Operations-Users
```
The group received:
```text
Change
Read
```
Full Control was intentionally not granted.
### NTFS Permissions
The same security group received NTFS permissions including:
```text
Modify
Read & execute
List folder contents
Read
Write
```
This created the authorization chain:
```text
jreed
   ↓
Operations-Users
   ↓
Share Permissions
   +
NTFS Permissions
   ↓
\\WS01\Operations
```
This demonstrated the principle of granting resource permissions to groups rather than individual user accounts.

