## 5. Network Share Access Verification
From AC02, Jordan accessed:
```text
\\WS01\Operations
```
The existing welcome document could be opened successfully.
To verify Modify permissions, Jordan:
- Created a new file.
- Opened the file.
- Modified its contents.
- Saved the file.
- Deleted the file.
All operations succeeded.
This verified that the combined Share and NTFS permissions were functioning as intended.

## 6. Automatic Drive Mapping with Group Policy
Created a new Group Policy Object:
```text
Operations - Mapped Drive
```
The GPO was linked to the Operations OU.
A Group Policy Preference was configured under:
```text
User Configuration
└── Preferences
    └── Windows Settings
        └── Drive Maps
```
Configuration:
```text
Action:       Update
Location:     \\WS01\Operations
Label:        Operations
Drive Letter: O:
```
On AC02, Group Policy was refreshed:
```cmd
gpupdate /force
```
Jordan then signed out and signed back in.
File Explorer automatically displayed:
```text
Operations (\\WS01\Operations) (O:)
```
The files remained accessible through the mapped drive.
This demonstrated that **drive mapping and resource authorization are separate functions**:
```text
Group Policy
    ↓
Maps O:

Operations-Users
    ↓
Grants permission to the contents
```
A mapped drive does not itself grant access to the underlying files.

## 7. Controlled Help-Desk Incident
A simulated support ticket was created:
> Jordan Reed reports that the Operations drive is visible but can no longer be accessed.
To create the failure, Jordan was intentionally removed from:
```text
Operations-Users
```
Jordan then completely signed out of AC02 and signed back in so that a new Windows security token would be generated.
The following command was used:
```cmd
whoami /groups
```
`Operations-Users` was no longer present.
However, the O: drive remained mapped.
Attempting to access the drive resulted in an access-denied message.
This produced the intended condition:
```text
Mapped drive:       Present
GPO:                Working
Domain login:       Working
Resource access:    Failed
Group membership:   Missing
```

## 8. Troubleshooting the Access Failure
Rather than immediately changing permissions, the failure was investigated from the client side.
Mapped network connections were inspected:
```cmd
net use
```
The O: drive remained mapped to:
```text
\\WS01\Operations
```
Group Policy application was inspected:
```cmd
gpresult /r
```
The mapped-drive GPO remained applicable to Jordan's user account.
The troubleshooting evidence showed:
```text
Can user authenticate?               Yes
Can user log into the domain?        Yes
Is O: mapped?                        Yes
Is the mapping GPO functioning?      Yes
Can the user access O:?              No
Is Operations-Users in user token?   No
```
The issue was therefore isolated to **authorization/group membership**, rather than DNS, network connectivity, authentication, Group Policy, or the drive mapping itself.
### Root Cause
Jordan had been removed from the security group that provided access to the Operations share.
### Corrective Action
Jordan was added back to:
```text
Operations-Users
```
The user completely signed out and signed back into AC02.
Access to O: was restored.
Jordan could once again read, create, edit, and delete files.

## 9. Additional Security Token / Kerberos Troubleshooting
After restoring the group membership, an unexpected behavior was observed.
Active Directory correctly showed Jordan as a member of `Operations-Users`, and Jordan could successfully modify files on the Operations share.
However:
```cmd
whoami /groups
```
did not initially display `Operations-Users`.
Additional verification was performed.
From AC02:
```cmd
net user jreed /domain
```
showed Jordan as a member of:
```text
Operations-Users
```
From WS01:
```powershell
Get-ADGroupMember "Operations-Users"
```
also confirmed Jordan's membership.
This produced an interesting discrepancy:
```text
Active Directory membership:   Correct
Domain account query:          Correct
Resource access:               Working
Current token reporting:       Unexpected
```
The current Kerberos state was inspected using:
```cmd
klist
```
Kerberos tickets were then purged:
```cmd
klist purge
```
Jordan completely signed out of Windows and signed back in.
The security groups were checked again:
```cmd
whoami /groups | findstr /I "Operations"
```
`Operations-Users` now appeared correctly.
### Troubleshooting Lesson
This demonstrated that Active Directory state and a user's current Windows session are not always identical.
Group membership changes may require authentication/session state to be refreshed before every diagnostic tool reflects the updated authorization state.
It also reinforced the importance of comparing multiple sources of evidence rather than assuming a single command tells the entire story.

## 10. Employee Offboarding
A simulated employee offboarding request was performed for Jordan Reed.
Rather than immediately deleting the AD account, the account was disabled.
Jordan was also removed from:
```text
Operations-Users
```
The default Domain Users membership was retained.
Resulting state:
```text
jreed
├── Account: Disabled
├── Domain Users: Retained
└── Operations-Users: Removed
```
Account status was verified using PowerShell:
```powershell
Get-ADUser jreed -Properties Enabled,MemberOf |
    Select-Object SamAccountName,Enabled,MemberOf
```
The account reported:
```text
SamAccountName : jreed
Enabled        : False
```
Jordan was completely signed out of AC02.
A subsequent login attempt using:
```text
BLACKWOOD\jreed
```
was rejected as expected.
This verified that the disabled account could no longer authenticate.

## 11. Why Disable Instead of Immediately Delete?
Disabling an account provides an immediate and reversible method of preventing authentication while preserving the Active Directory object.
In a production organization, account retention may be necessary for:
- Auditing
- Data retention
- File ownership
- Investigations
- Organizational policy
- Recovery from an incorrect offboarding request
Actual offboarding procedures and retention periods depend on organizational policy.
The lab therefore used a **disable-first** approach rather than immediately deleting the account.

